{
  "opportunity_id": "proc_74b0718acf",
  "source": "content_cluster",
  "title": "Metered gas that flowed with no deal: HPL Logistics write-off requests",
  "task_class": "deal_entry_correction",
  "label_mechanical": "Deal entry and correction in Sitara / Unify / TAGG: resolve meter / deal / flow",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "A meter on HPL's system records gas that flowed on days where no deal covers the volume, and Logistics cannot record it. Someone in Logistics mails the desk a per-meter, per-month write-up naming the days, the volumes and the expired or missing deal number, then asks the recipient to choose one of a standard set of remedies: extend the existing deal, issue a new deal, or approve a write-off to Unaccounted for Gas. The recipient is expected to reply with a deal number or an approval; in this set only a small number of replies are visible, one of them supplying a deal number and one escalating because the named person did not recognise the deal. The message body is written by hand each time even though the three-option wording is near-identical across instances, and the same template recurs meter by meter.",
    "where_it_stalls": "The sender cannot close the volume alone \u2014 the fix requires a trader or deal owner to extend a contract, cut a new deal, or authorise a write-off, and almost every message in this cluster is an outbound request with almost no visible reply. Where a response does appear, it is either a deal number supplied late or an escalation reporting that the person asked was unaware of the deal and speculating it might relate to ENTEX. The volumes are frequently months old by the time they surface (contracts expired the prior August or September), so the work is reconstruction after the fact rather than correction at the point of flow.",
    "why_it_recurs": "Contracts expire while gas keeps physically flowing at the meter, and nothing links the deal's end date to the meter's measurement, so every accounting cycle throws up a fresh batch of uncovered volumes to be chased by hand.",
    "automation_opportunity": "A scheduled comparison of measured meter flow against active deal coverage that flags uncovered meter-days as they occur and raises a pre-filled request naming the meter, days, volumes and the expired deal, with the three standard dispositions as selectable options routed to the deal owner and logged when answered. Contract expiry dates could also trigger an advance warning at meters still recording flow.",
    "coherence": "tight"
  },
